# [Scenario] Users lose their order-list filters when they visit a detail page and come back. They also want to share a filtered view with a teammate. How do you fix both?

## 1. What is it?

Two complaints, but really just one problem:

1. A user picks some filters on the orders list (say, "Shipped" orders), clicks into one order to see details, then clicks back — and the filters are gone. The list is back to showing everything.
2. A user wants to send a coworker a link that opens the list already filtered the same way they're seeing it. Right now there's no way to do that.

Both problems go away with one simple idea: **keep the filters in the URL itself**, not in some variable inside the component.

So instead of the page just being `/orders`, it becomes `/orders?status=shipped&q=acme`. The moment you do that:

- Going to a detail page and coming back doesn't lose anything, because the filters were never "stored" anywhere fragile — they're sitting right there in the address bar the whole time.
- Sharing is automatic. Copy the link, send it, done. The filters travel with the link because they *are* part of the link.

## 2. Why is this the right fix?

It helps to see why the more obvious ideas don't fully work.

**Idea 1: store the filter in a normal variable on the component** (like `selectedStatus = 'shipped'`). This fails because Angular normally throws away a component when you navigate to a different page and builds a fresh one when you come back. Any variable living on that old component is just gone. This is exactly the bug being reported.

**Idea 2: store the filter in a shared Angular service** (something marked `providedIn: 'root'`, so it isn't destroyed when the component is). This one actually *does* survive going to detail and back — services stick around. But it only lives inside that one person's browser tab. If they refresh the page, or a teammate opens the list fresh, the filter is gone. So this fixes complaint #1 but not complaint #2.

**Idea 3: store it in `localStorage`.** This survives even a page refresh. But it's still stuck on one person's computer — it can't be shared with anyone else, and worse, it can quietly show someone filters from three days ago that they forgot they set, which is confusing.

Notice the pattern: every one of these fixes only one of the two complaints. The URL is the only place that naturally solves both, because a URL is two things at once: it's part of your browser's back/forward history (so leaving and returning restores it), and it's a plain piece of text you can copy and send to anyone (so sharing is free).

Put another way: `/orders?status=shipped` and `/orders?status=pending` really are two different "pages" from the user's point of view, the same way `/orders/5` and `/orders/9` are two different pages. Treating the filter as part of the address, not as hidden internal state, matches how people already think about it.

## 3. How does it work?

- The list component never keeps its own private copy of "what filter is currently selected." Instead, it always reads the current filter straight from the URL, using Angular's `ActivatedRoute.queryParamMap`.
- When the user changes a filter, the component doesn't save that choice anywhere itself — it just tells the router to change the URL, using `router.navigate([], { queryParams: {...}, queryParamsHandling: 'merge' })`. That URL change is what the component sees and reacts to, which is what makes the list re-filter.

//////
So who "changes the route"? The component itself does — through Router

It's not some external, mysterious force — the same component that reads the filter is also the one that triggers the URL change, using Router.navigate(). The reason this design still counts as "not storing state locally" is: the component never keeps selectedFilter = 'electronics' sitting in its own variable to represent the current selection. Instead, it says "go change the URL to say this," and then goes back to reading whatever the URL currently says, via the separate ActivatedRoute.queryParamMap stream. The URL becomes the actual home of the value — the component just writes to it and reads from it, rather than owning a private copy.

Why bother routing through the URL instead of just setting a variable directly

Because Router.navigate() doesn't just quietly update some internal value — it does a real browser navigation: it pushes a new entry onto browser history (so back/forward works) and changes the actual address bar (so refresh/share/bookmark work). A plain this.selectedFilter = 'electronics' would update your component's memory instantly, but none of those other browser behaviors, because it never touches the URL at all.
//////

So the flow is: **user does something → the URL changes → the URL is what the page reads to decide what to show.** There's only one place the filter lives — the address bar — so there's nothing that can ever get out of sync with anything else, because there's no "anything else."

Compare that to the more obvious way of doing it: save the filter in a variable, *and* also try to keep the URL updated to match it. Now there are two copies of the same fact, and every time you change one, you have to remember to change the other too. Forget once, and they disagree — the dropdown says "Shipped" but the link you copy says nothing, so the link you share is wrong. Making the URL the *only* copy means that specific bug simply can't happen, because there's no second copy to fall out of sync.

A few small but important details:

- `queryParamsHandling: 'merge'` means: when you update the status filter, don't erase other filters that are also sitting in the URL, like a search term or page number. Just add or update the one you're changing.
- `replaceUrl: true` means: when the filter changes, swap the current browser-history entry instead of adding a new one. Without this, every single click or keystroke would create a new "back" step, and the back button would become useless (you'd have to click it 20 times to actually leave the page).
- For carrying filters into the detail page and back out again, you don't need to write any code for that at all — Angular's `routerLink` has a built-in setting, `queryParamsHandling="preserve"`, that means "whatever query params are currently in the URL, keep them when you navigate to this link." Put that on the "View" link (list → detail) and on the "Back to Orders" link (detail → list), and the filters just travel along automatically.
- Angular normally destroys the list component and builds a new one every time you leave and return to that route. Normally that's the source of the bug. With this design, it doesn't matter, because the new component doesn't need to remember anything — it just reads the URL again, the same way it did the first time.

## 4. Example

**Live, working demo:** https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-xidl6gmg-zirnzrqs?file=src%2Fapp%2Forders-list%2Forders-list.component.html

The demo has a list of 8 orders, a status dropdown, a search box, and a "View" link into a detail page with a "Back to Orders" link.

## 5. Example Explanation – Execution Flow

Here's what actually happens, step by step, in the live demo:

1. You open `/orders`. No filters yet, all 8 orders show up.
2. You pick "Shipped" from the dropdown. Behind the scenes, this calls `router.navigate` with the new status. The URL becomes `/orders?status=shipped`. The table instantly narrows down to just the 3 shipped orders. A "shareable link" box on the page also updates to show this new URL.
3. You click "View" on one of the orders. Because that link is set up with `queryParamsHandling="preserve"`, it carries the current filter along, so the URL becomes `/orders/1?status=shipped` — not just `/orders/1`.
4. The detail page reads that `status` value straight out of its own URL and displays it ("Filters carried through navigation: status = shipped"). No code passed that value between the two pages directly — it rode along inside the URL.
5. You click "← Back to Orders." That link also has `queryParamsHandling="preserve"`, so it carries the same `status=shipped` back with it. The URL becomes `/orders?status=shipped` again. Angular has built a brand-new list component from scratch at this point — but it doesn't matter, because that new component just reads `status=shipped` from the URL immediately and shows the same filtered list the user left behind.
6. If, at step 2 or step 3, the user had instead copied that URL and sent it to a teammate, opening it would show that exact same filtered (or filtered-plus-detail) view. Nothing extra had to be built for sharing — it's the same mechanism doing double duty.

## 5b. A Second Way to Build This: Reactive Forms Instead of Plain Inputs

The example above wires up the dropdown and search box with simple `[ngModel]` bindings and calls `router.navigate()` directly every time something changes. That works, but in a real app with more filters, or a search box that shouldn't fire on every single keystroke, Reactive Forms is usually a better fit, because it gives you the input's changes as an RxJS stream you can control (like adding a short delay before reacting).

**Live, working demo (Reactive Forms version):** https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-xidl6gmg-zirnzrqs

The one thing that gets trickier: now there are technically two things that need to agree — the form on screen, and the URL — instead of just one. So the code needs to keep them in sync in *both* directions, and there's one detail that's easy to miss but important: `emitEvent: false`.

```ts
import { Component, OnDestroy, inject, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { FormControl, FormGroup, ReactiveFormsModule } from '@angular/forms';
import { ActivatedRoute, Router, NavigationEnd } from '@angular/router';
import { Subject } from 'rxjs';
import { debounceTime, distinctUntilChanged, filter, takeUntil } from 'rxjs/operators';
import { ORDERS } from '../models/order';

@Component({ /* ... */ })
export class OrdersListComponent implements OnDestroy {
  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute);
  private router = inject(Router);
  private destroy$ = new Subject<void>();

  orders = ORDERS;

  // This is just the on-screen form. It is kept in sync with the URL below,
  // in both directions - it is not treated as the "real" source of truth by itself.
  filterForm = new FormGroup({
    status: new FormControl<string>('all', { nonNullable: true }),
    q: new FormControl<string>('', { nonNullable: true }),
  });

  filteredOrders = signal(this.orders);
  shareableUrl = signal(window.location.href);

  constructor() {
    // Direction 1: URL updates the form.
    // Runs immediately with whatever's in the URL right now, and again every
    // time the URL changes later (browser back button, a shared link, or our
    // own code below). The { emitEvent: false } part matters a lot - without
    // it, this update would itself look like a "user changed the form" event,
    // which would trigger direction 2 below, which would change the URL again,
    // which would trigger this again... forever.
    this.route.queryParamMap
      .pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$))
      .subscribe((params) => {
        const status = params.get('status') ?? 'all';
        const q = params.get('q') ?? '';
        this.filterForm.patchValue({ status, q }, { emitEvent: false });
        this.applyFilters(status, q);
      });

    // Direction 2: the form updates the URL.
    // Handled separately per field on purpose. The dropdown is one discrete
    // click, so it updates the URL right away. The text box is typed
    // character by character, so it waits 300ms after the user stops typing
    // before updating the URL - otherwise every keystroke would trigger a
    // navigation.
    this.filterForm.controls.status.valueChanges
      .pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$), distinctUntilChanged())
      .subscribe(() => this.pushFiltersToUrl());

    this.filterForm.controls.q.valueChanges
      .pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$), debounceTime(300), distinctUntilChanged())
      .subscribe(() => this.pushFiltersToUrl());

    this.router.events
      .pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$), filter((e): e is NavigationEnd => e instanceof NavigationEnd))
      .subscribe(() => this.shareableUrl.set(window.location.href));
  }

  ngOnDestroy(): void {
    this.destroy$.next();
    this.destroy$.complete();
  }

  private pushFiltersToUrl(): void {
    const { status, q } = this.filterForm.getRawValue();
    this.router.navigate([], {
      relativeTo: this.route,
      queryParams: { status: status === 'all' ? null : status, q: q || null },
      queryParamsHandling: 'merge',
      replaceUrl: true,
    });
  }

  private applyFilters(status: string, search: string): void {
    const s = search.toLowerCase();
    this.filteredOrders.set(
      this.orders.filter((o) => {
        const matchesStatus = status === 'all' || o.status === status;
        const matchesSearch = !s || o.customer.toLowerCase().includes(s);
        return matchesStatus && matchesSearch;
      }),
    );
  }
}
```

```html
<form [formGroup]="filterForm">
  <select formControlName="status">
    <option value="all">All</option>
    <option value="shipped">Shipped</option>
    <!-- ... -->
  </select>
  <input type="text" formControlName="q" placeholder="Search customer..." />
</form>
```


//// this is how the code is working
Read it top to bottom, ignoring the RxJS operator names for a second:

this.route.queryParamMap — this is a stream that tells you the current URL's query params, and re-announces itself every time they change.
.subscribe((params) => {...}) — "whenever that happens, run this code."
Inside: params.get('status') — pull the status value out of the URL (e.g. from ?status=active, this gives 'active'). If it's missing, default to 'all'.
Same for q (the search text).
this.filterForm.patchValue({ status, q }, ...) — take those two values and write them into the form. So if the URL says status=active, the dropdown in the UI now shows "active" — the form visually updates to match the URL.
this.applyFilters(status, q) — actually go filter the displayed list using those values.

In plain English: "Whenever the URL's query params change (for any reason), read them out, and update both the form UI and the filtered list to match."

////////

**Why `emitEvent: false` matters:** Picture what happens without it. The URL changes, so the code updates the form to match. But updating the form normally counts as "the form changed," which fires off `valueChanges`, which is exactly the code that pushes the form's value back into the URL. So the URL update would trigger a form update, which would trigger another URL update, which would trigger another form update — an endless loop, all happening instantly, freezing the page. `emitEvent: false` just means "update this control quietly, don't tell anyone it changed." That breaks the loop: only real, actual user typing or clicking ever triggers a URL update.

**Why the two fields are handled separately instead of watching the whole form at once:** because they need different behavior. A dropdown selection is one clean action — react to it immediately. A text box is typed one letter at a time — reacting immediately would mean firing off a URL change (and a browser history-adjacent update) after every single letter, which is wasteful and would make the URL flicker constantly while someone is mid-word.

From the user's point of view, this version behaves exactly like the simpler one — same URL-driven memory, same filters-follow-you-into-and-out-of-the-detail-page behavior, same shareable link. It's just built with `FormGroup` instead of individual `[ngModel]` bindings, which holds up better once you have more than a couple of filters, or once any of them need that typing delay.

## 6. When to use?

- Any list, table, or search page with filters, sorting, or pagination that people would reasonably expect to still be there after they click away and come back.
- Any view your team wants people to be able to bookmark or send to each other — a filtered support queue, a saved report, a specific search result.
- Skip this approach for anything private or sensitive. Don't put a password, a security token, or something personally sensitive into a URL — URLs get logged, saved in browser history, and pasted into chat windows without a second thought. Keep this pattern for filter/sort/search criteria, not for secrets.

## 7. Advantages / Disadvantages

**Advantages**

- One design fixes both problems (lost filters, and no way to share) instead of needing two separate features.
- The browser's own back and forward buttons just work correctly, for free.
- The page becomes bookmarkable and linkable, which is also handy for things like "here's exactly what I'm seeing, come look" support requests.
- No special code is needed to "restore" state when the component gets rebuilt, because the component was never relying on surviving in the first place.

**Disadvantages**

- Anyone can see and edit the query params by hand (they're right there in the address bar), so the code has to handle a weird or broken value gracefully instead of crashing — a typo'd `?status=xyz` should just fall back to "show everything," not break the page.
- It's not a good fit for large or sensitive information — don't stuff an entire complicated object as raw text into the URL, and never put anything sensitive there.
- Every filter change becomes a URL change, so without `replaceUrl: true` (and a short delay on typing) you can end up spamming the browser's history or triggering more work than necessary.

## 8. Common Interview Traps

- Reaching straight for "put it in a shared service" or "put it in localStorage" as *the* answer. Both of those fix losing filters on navigation — neither one fixes sharing. A strong answer notices out loud that these are two requirements, and picks the one idea that happens to satisfy both.
- Not knowing that `queryParamsHandling="preserve"` already exists as a built-in option on `routerLink`. Some people reinvent this by hand — manually reading the current query params and re-attaching them to every link — which works, but is unnecessary extra code for something the router already does declaratively.
- Not thinking about history spam: calling `router.navigate` on every single keystroke without `replaceUrl: true` (or a typing delay) fills up the back button with dozens of near-identical stops.
- Assuming Angular keeps a routed component alive by default when you navigate away and back. It doesn't, unless you set up a custom reuse strategy. The right response to "the component gets rebuilt" isn't to fight that — it's to stop relying on the component remembering anything at all, and read everything fresh from the URL instead.
- Forgetting to handle a bad or missing query param gracefully — a shared link with a typo in it should fall back to sensible defaults, not show a blank list or throw an error.

## 9. Interview Follow-up Questions

**Q1: What if the filter is too complicated to reasonably fit in a URL — like 15 selected checkboxes, or a date range?**
A: Keep the URL short and readable — use something compact like comma-separated IDs or short date strings, rather than dumping a big raw object into it. For genuinely complex saved views, a common pattern is to save the full filter definition on the server against a short ID, and only put that ID in the URL, like `?view=abc123`. Many reporting and analytics tools work exactly this way.

**Q2: How do you stop the search box from triggering a URL change on every single keystroke?**
A: Add a short delay (RxJS `debounceTime`, commonly 300ms) so the code waits until the user pauses typing before updating the URL, and keep using `replaceUrl: true` so even those delayed updates don't spam browser history.

**Q3: Does this only work for filtering data that's already loaded in the browser, or does it work with a real backend too?**
A: It works either way. Whether the filtering happens in the browser (like this simplified demo) or the query params are used to build a request to a server (`GET /api/orders?status=shipped&page=2`), the URL is still the one thing that decides what gets shown or fetched.

**Q4: What happens if the user hits the browser's back or forward button instead of clicking something in the app?**
A: Nothing special has to be done for that case specifically — because the component always reads its filter from the URL rather than from its own separate memory, any URL change at all (whether from a click in the app, a browser back/forward, or someone pasting in a link) automatically updates what's shown. There's no extra syncing step required, because there was never a second copy of the state to keep in sync.

## 10. Quick Answer (say this first)

"Both problems come from the same root cause and share one fix: store the filters in the URL's query params instead of in a component variable or a service. That way, leaving and returning to the page doesn't lose anything, because the URL never gets wiped — and sharing is automatic, because copying the link copies the filtered view with it. Angular's `routerLink` has a built-in `queryParamsHandling=\"preserve\"` option that carries those params into and out of a detail page with no extra code."

## 11. Interview-Ready Answer (full)

"Both complaints actually point to the same fix: make the URL's query parameters the source of truth for the list's filters, instead of storing them in a component variable or a shared service. When someone changes a filter, I don't save that choice anywhere myself — I just ask the router to update the URL, using `queryParamsHandling: 'merge'` so it doesn't wipe out other filters like a page number, and `replaceUrl: true` so we're not spamming the browser's history on every small change. The list component always reads its current filter from `ActivatedRoute.queryParamMap` rather than from its own memory, so even though Angular tears down and rebuilds the component when you go to a detail page and come back, it doesn't matter — the new component just reads the same URL and rebuilds the same view. For carrying filters into the detail page and back out, Angular's router already has a built-in way to do that with no extra code: the `routerLink` directive's `queryParamsHandling=\"preserve\"` attribute, on both the link going into the detail page and the link coming back. And because the filters live entirely in the URL, sharing a filtered view is just sharing that link — a teammate opening it sees exactly the same filtered list. Same mechanism solves both asks."

## 12. Quick Revision

1. **Problem:** filters get lost after visiting a detail page and coming back, and there's no way to share a filtered view.
2. **Root cause:** Angular destroys and rebuilds a component when you navigate away and back, so anything stored only on that component is lost. A shared service or localStorage would survive that, but can't be shared with someone else.
3. **Fix:** store filters in the URL's query params instead. The URL survives navigation (it's part of browser history) and is shareable by nature (it's just a copyable string).
4. **How:** read filters from `ActivatedRoute.queryParamMap`; write changes with `router.navigate(..., { queryParamsHandling: 'merge', replaceUrl: true })`; carry filters into/out of a detail page with `routerLink`'s `queryParamsHandling="preserve"` — no manual code needed for that part.
5. **Watch out for:** validating a bad/missing query param instead of crashing; not putting sensitive data in the URL; debouncing free-text search so it doesn't spam navigations.

## 13. Memory Trick

**The URL is the save file.** Instead of the app trying to remember what you were looking at (in memory, which gets erased the moment the page component is torn down), the address bar itself holds the save — which is exactly why bookmarks and shared links work on the web at all. Once you see the URL as the save file, both complaints in this scenario turn out to be the same missing feature: the app just wasn't saving to it.
